/-!
# Finite spaces denoted by Gavrilovich diagrams

A diagram box such as `⌜a → b⌟` elaborates to `Space L E`, where

* `L : List (List String)` lists the points, each as a sorted list of its glued names
  (`a=b` is `["a", "b"]`), and
* `E : List (ℕ × ℕ)` lists specialization edges as pairs of indices into `L`.

More generally, names can have any type `α` (with `DecidableEq α` to look points up by name, and
`ToString α` to print them), e.g. pairs of names for the points of a product.

`Space L E` is the finite type `Pts L E` with the preorder generated by `E`, carrying the
upper-set (Alexandrov) topology. An edge `(i, j)` means that point `j` is in the closure of
point `i`, i.e. `i ⤳ j`, and so (as for Mathlib's `specializationPreorder`) that `j ≤ i`: the
arrows of a diagram point down the order. For example, `⌜T → F⌟` is ordered as `Prop` is, with
`F ≤ T`, and `T` is its open point.

The elaborator always emits `E` as the full irreflexive transitive closure, sorted, so different
drawings of the same space are syntactically equal. Nothing here depends on that, however: the
order is the reflexive-transitive closure of whatever `E` is given, and deciding it uses a
reachability check (`reach`) that is proved correct once.

## Main definitions

* `Gavrilovich.Pts L E`: the points of the space.
* `Gavrilovich.Space L E`: the space, as an object of `TopCat`.
* `Gavrilovich.Space.homOfTable`: the continuous map given by a table of indices,
  with monotonicity checked by `decide`.
* `Gavrilovich.Space.pt`: the point with a given name. The notation `pt(o)` is
  this in patterns, e.g. `fun | pt(o) => x | pt(c) => y`, and its canonical form `Space.mk k _`
  as a term.
* `Gavrilovich.Space.opens`: all open sets, as a `Finset`. `Space.mem_opens` shows
  that it is complete, and `Space.forall_isOpen`/`Space.exists_isOpen` turn quantifiers over
  open sets into (decidable) quantifiers over `opens`. Open sets of `Finset`s and other sets with
  decidable membership are decidable directly, so `decide` proves `IsOpen {pt(o)}`.
* `Gavrilovich.Space.closeds`: all closed sets, with the same API.
  `Space.down x` is the closure of `{x}` (`Space.coe_down`), and `Space.up x` the smallest open
  set containing `x`.

## Implementation notes

`Pts` is a `structure`, not a type synonym for `Fin L.length`. With a synonym, the unifier would
unfold `Pts L E =?= Pts L' E'` to `Fin n =?= Fin n` and silently identify differently labelled
spaces. This also lets us assign it to an arbitrary universe, which is useful for interop with
existing spaces.

`Space` is a `def`, not an `abbrev`. As an `abbrev`, `simp` would reduce the type of points
`↑(Space L E)` (that is, `TopCat.carrier (TopCat.of (WithUpperSet (Pts L E)))`) to
`WithUpperSet (Pts L E)`, e.g. in the types of the coercions of morphisms to functions, while the
discrimination tree keys lemmas on the unreduced form, so that lemmas about points would not fire.
Since it still unfolds at default transparency, pattern matching on points, `rfl` and `decide`
see through it. Instances are declared on `Space L E` directly. Morphisms are best built with
`ConcreteCategory.ofHom` (or `Space.hom`) rather than `TopCat.ofHom`: the latter is
`ConcreteCategory.ofHom` between `TopCat.of X`s, and the unifier finds `X` by unfolding `Space`.
-/


